    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Zengi's capture of the County of Edessa after the siege of 1144 made him a hero in the Muslim world; he was assassinated by a slave two years later.</li><li>Nur ad-Din took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from the Burids in 1154 and, through his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> and Shirkuh's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, brought Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under Zengid dominion in 1169.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> crushed the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> at the Horns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> on 13 April 1175 and received Aleppo on 12 June 1183, ending Zengid rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> took Amid from the Zengid coalition in April 1183, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> counted among its stores 80,000 candles, a tower full of arrowheads and 1,040,000 books.</p></aside>
    </section>

