nk" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> after the siege of Edessa, a feat that makes him a hero in the Muslim world.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1154</span><span class="rf-text">Nur ad-Din captures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from the Turkic Burid dynasty and rules <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from there.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1169</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> kills the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Shawar and seizes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as Fatimid vizier in Nur ad-Din's name; on his death the same year his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> succeeds him.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1174</span><span class="rf-text">Nur ad-Din dies on 15 May; his eleven-year-old son as-Salih Ismail inherits, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> on 23 November.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1183</span><span class="rf-text">Imad ad-Din Zangi hands <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> on 12 June in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a>, Nusaybin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>, ending Zengid rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1234</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a>, atabeg to the child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, takes the city in his own name, possibly after murdering the last Zengid emir Nasir ad-Din Mahmud.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">