    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers.jpg" alt="Raymond of Poitiers" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers">Raymond of Poitiers</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raymond_of_Poitiers_with_John_II_Komnenos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zengids were a Turkoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim dynasty that began as an atabegate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>. In 1127, after the murder of Aqsunqur al-Bursuqi, atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, the Seljuks named Zengi, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-sunqur-al-hajib">Aq Sunqur al-Hajib</a>, their former governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, as the new atabeg; he had already been a successful Seljuk general and governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Zengi quickly became the chief Turkic potentate of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and Iraq, taking Aleppo from the squabbling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a> in 1128 and, after the siege of Edessa in 1144, capturing the County of Edessa from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, a feat that made him a hero in the Muslim world. In 1146 he was assassinated by a slave, and his lands were divided: Mosul and Iraq to his eldest son Saif ad-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> I, Aleppo and Edessa to his second son Nur ad-Din.</p><p>Nur ad-Din proved as competent as his father. He defeated the Crusaders at Edessa in 1146, beat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers">Raymond of Poitiers</a>, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, at the battle of Inab in 1149, took the remnants of the County of Edessa west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in 1150, and in 1154 captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from the Turkic Burid dynasty. After the Seljuk defeats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Ahmad Sanjar</a> in 1157 the Zengids remained Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in name but became essentially independent, and the house branched into lines at Mosul, Aleppo, Damascus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a>, al-Jazira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrizor">Shahrazur</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At their widest in 1174 the Zengid lands ran from Tripoli to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> held in Nur ad-Din's name and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. Their towns were centres of industry and letters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> developed in the early thirteenth century a flourishing industry of luxury brass inlaid with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, a technique that had come west from Khurasan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. Mosul brasswork bears more inscriptions than any other class of medieval Islamic object, and from 1220 to 1240 the craft saw “rapid innovations in technique, decoration, and composition”. The geographer Ibn Sa'id noted around 1250 “many crafts in the city, especially inlaid brass vessels which are exported (and presented) to rulers”, goods only the wealthiest could afford.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>'s wealth showed in the citadel stores <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> bought from the departing Zangi in 1183, and at Amid that year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> took 80,000 candles, a tower of arrowheads and 1,040,000 books. The region's Christians lived through a general decline under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabisation">Arabisation</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a>, yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> passed through a “Syriac <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>” in which discriminatory rules were lifted, especially after the death of the conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> in 1174, and illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> were made at Mar Mattai near Mosul around 1220. The Kitab al-Diryaq of 1198–1199 and the Kitab al-Aghani of 1218–1219 belong to an “explosion of figural art” shared with Syriac <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon.jpg" alt="Raynald of Châtillon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ReynaldofChatillon%26PatriarchofAntioch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zengid army continued Seljuk traditions, combining professional askar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> troops with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and Turcoman and Kurdish tribal auxiliaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>'s army was mainly Turcoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> and Kurdish spear-armed horsemen, with professional ghulams, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> cavalry and large bodies of infantry; the ruler's askar numbered 1,000 to 3,000 and the auxiliaries 10,000 to 15,000. Dense arrow volleys could pierce armour and stop a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> charge, and the Zengids were skilled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare">siege warfare</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> and his successors kept the model.</p><p>Their fame rested on war with the Crusader states: Zengi's capture of Edessa in 1144, Nur ad-Din's victory at Inab in 1149 and the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a>. From 1163 Nur ad-Din contested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric of Jerusalem</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a>, with his nephew Saladin, won the Battle of al-Babein in 1167, relieved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1168 and in 1169 killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Shawar and seized Egypt for Nur ad-Din. The dynasty then fought its own protégé. At the Horns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> on 13 April 1175 Saladin's outnumbered veterans surrounded and crushed the combined armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>; at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s Mound in 1176 the Zengids broke his left wing before his charge routed them, and Saif ad-Din barely escaped. Saladin's siege of Mosul in 1182 stalled, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a> fell after fifteen days, and the coalition that met him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in February 1183 asked for peace: “like men they advanced, like women they vanished”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bimaristan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bimaristan.jpg" alt="Bimaristan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bimaristan">Bimaristan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bimaristan_Argun_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zengids ruled as atabegs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> under a nominal Seljuk suzerainty until 1194 and then as a Seljuk successor-state. Power was divided among the family by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>, and the head of the house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> regarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> as the family estate; minors ruled through guardians, as the eleven-year-old as-Salih Ismail did at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> under the emir Gumushtigin from 1174. Nur ad-Din governed as a builder of institutions: his funerary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> of 1167, the first such complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimaristan">Bimaristan</a>, a hospital, in 1154; the Great Mosque of al-Nuri at Mosul in 1172–1173; and at Aleppo the fortified citadel, rebuilt walls, a palace and a racecourse.</p><p>Religion legitimised rule. The Zengids were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> in Nur ad-Din's name until Saladin, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adid">al-Adid</a>'s death in 1171, switched Egypt to the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. The caliphate arbitrated the family's later wars: Mosul appealed to an-Nasir against Saladin in 1182, while Saladin's letters accused the Zengids of backing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and obstructing the holy war. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> Saladin suppressed as-Salih's name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a> and on coins; the truce of 1176 left as-Salih in Aleppo as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and the treaty of 1186 left Mosul to Mas'ud on condition of military support. In Mosul the commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a>, made atabeg in 1211, ruled through three child emirs and murdered the last in 1234.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu.jpg" alt="Badr al-Din Lu'lu'" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badr_al_din_lu%27lu%27_(frontispiece).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">13th century painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zengid atabegate became famous in the Islamic world for its successes against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> and as the house from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> emerged. Zengi's capture of Edessa in 1144 made him a hero; Nur ad-Din's unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> held back the Crusaders' repeated assaults on both cities, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a>'s seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1169 brought the Fatimid state under Zengid dominion. The Zengid military model of askar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a> and tribal cavalry passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>, and the yellow eagle banner Saladin flew was apparently inherited from the Zengids. Saladin's own career was framed by his old masters: he hesitated to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> because taking a land that had belonged to his master was forbidden by the Islamic principles he held, and he waited eight years for Aleppo, “the eye of Syria”.</p><p>Culturally the dynasty's cities set standards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> was the leading centre of inlaid brass in the early thirteenth century, and the Mosul painters' Kitab al-Diryaq and Kitab al-Aghani stand near the beginning of Arabic illustrated books. Nur ad-Din's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and hospital in Damascus and his mosque at Mosul remained in use, and his fortification of the citadel of Aleppo, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joscelin-ii-of-edessa">Joscelin II of Edessa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</a> were held, shaped one of the great castles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Under the late Zengids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>” produced illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> that are now a reference for the arms and armour of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aleppo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleppo.jpg" alt="Aleppo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleppo_Citadel_entrance_-_seen_from_southeast_9933.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zengids were undone by the man they made. When Nur ad-Din died on 15 May 1174 his power passed to his eleven-year-old son as-Salih Ismail al-Malik, taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> under the emir Gumushtigin. Gumushtigin's bid to unseat his rivals drove the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to call on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who entered Damascus on 23 November 1174 and defeated the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and Aleppo at the Horns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> in 1175 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s Mound in 1176. As-Salih kept Aleppo as Saladin's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> until his death in 1181, when it passed to Imad ad-Din Zangi. In 1182 Kukbary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> defected and invited Saladin into the Jazira: its cities fell one by one, and on 12 June 1183 Zangi handed Aleppo to Saladin for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a>, Nusaybin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>, ending Zengid rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p>Mosul held out. Saladin's last offensive in late 1185 failed before the city's resistance and his own illness, and the treaty of March 1186 left Mas'ud in Mosul bound to supply troops. Ayyubid attacks in 1204 and 1209 forced Nur ad-Din Arslan Shah I to acknowledge Ayyubid suzerainty on his coins. Dying with young sons, he made his commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a> their atabeg in 1211; Lu'lu' ruled through three child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> until 1234, when he took Mosul in his own name, and held it until 1259, accepting Mongol suzerainty after 1243. The last Zengid line, the emirs of al-Jazira, ended in 1250 when Mahmud al-Malik al-Zahir's lands fell to an-Nasir Yusuf, the Ayyubid emir of Aleppo.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Zengids rose from Zengi's appointment as Seljuk atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in 1127 to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, Edessa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, holding back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>; after Nur ad-Din's death in 1174 Saladin took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from them, and their last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> in Mosul and al-Jazira gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a> in 1234 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> in 1250.</em></p>

